You can adjust these using the custom CSS feature (Settings > Custom CSS). For your specific set up, I think this should be a good starting point:

```
/* Give proportional sizes to columns in the download client widget */ 
table.downloads-widget-table {
  # Job name
  --header-name-size: 70 !important;
  --col-name-size: 70 !important;

  # Progress
  --header-progress-size: 30 !important;
  --col-progress-size: 30 !important;

  # File size
  --header-size-size: 10 !important;
  --col-size-size: 10 !important;

  # Finish time
  --header-time-size: 20 !important;
  --col-time-size: 20 !important;
}
```

but you can adjust the numbers. A higher number will make that section larger (these values are relative to each other). Note that `--header-*` adjusts the size of  the header row, while `--col-*` adjusts the size of the rest of the rows.

Oops, the `# Column name` is messing up the line immediately after (mixed my programming languages up), so the header CSS isn't applying.

```
/* Give proportional sizes to columns in the download client widget */ 
table.downloads-widget-table {
  /* File name */
  --header-name-size: 70 !important;
  --col-name-size: 70 !important;

  /* Progress */
  --header-progress-size: 30 !important;
  --col-progress-size: 30 !important;

  /* File size */
  --header-size-size: 10 !important;
  --col-size-size: 10 !important;

  /* Finish time */
  --header-time-size: 20 !important;
  --col-time-size: 20 !important;
}
``` 

That should do it. You can also remove the [comments](https://www.w3schools.com/css/css_comments.asp) completely, since they're just there as notes.
